# modules/sast/rules/secrets.rules - docs/DESIGN.md §6.3 "secrets.rules" seed
# catalog: AWS keys, private-key headers, generic api_key=/password= literals,
# JWTs.  docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 9: every match here is exactly the kind of
# text the redaction pipeline (rules/redaction.rules) exists for.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ASSIGNMENT-FORM MATRIX, and why these patterns look the way they do
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The four credential-assignment records below (GENERIC_PASSWORD, GENERIC_API_KEY,
# GENERIC_SECRET, ENV_ASSIGNMENT) cover a deliberate matrix of assignment forms
# rather than the handful anyone happened to think of.  The matrix, the planted
# positive controls that pin each cell, and the negative controls that pin the
# precision budget all live in tests/fixtures/sast-secret-forms/, driven by
# tests/suites/sast-secrets-forms.sh.  Before this pack was widened, 7 of that
# tree's 47 positive controls were found and 40 were missed - every miss a
# credential the operator was told was not there.
#
# Case is spelled out as bracket classes ([Pp][Aa]...), never as a flag:
# rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §8.2 freezes `ere` as case-SENSITIVE and forbids inline
# flags `(?i)` outright, precisely so the same bytes mean the same thing under
# `grep -E` (BSD) and under `rg` (Rust).  `[Mm][Dd]5` in that section is the
# documented idiom; this is the same idiom at scale.  A `(?i)` here would be
# E040 and would not survive the BSD leg of tools/daily-suite.sh either way.
#
# TWO SHAPES, TWO DIFFERENT PRECISION BUDGETS.  This is the load-bearing design
# decision, and getting it backwards is what makes a secrets pack unusable:
#
#   * A QUOTED literal (GENERIC_PASSWORD / GENERIC_API_KEY / GENERIC_SECRET) is
#     matched ANYWHERE on a line.  The quotes are the evidence: they prove the
#     bytes are a literal string and not an identifier, a reference, or a call,
#     so `connect(password="...")` mid-line is a real finding and can be read as
#     one without any syntactic context.
#
#   * An UNQUOTED literal (ENV_ASSIGNMENT) is matched ONLY in line-anchored
#     assignment syntax - the `.env` / shell `export` / Dockerfile `ENV` /
#     compose-and-CI `KEY: value` shape.  Nothing proves an unquoted value is a
#     literal. JavaScript/TypeScript source is excluded for this record: an
#     anchored object property or default argument can still be a reference.
#     The quoted rules above retain their source coverage. Dropping the
#     anchor is not a widening, it is the difference between a usable pack and one that flags
#     `password: password` in `coll.findOne({ user: user, password: password })`
#     - a line that is already in tests/fixtures/clean/nosql_app.js.  A rule set
#     that flags every KEY=value line trains operators to ignore the tool, which
#     is the same outcome as missing the secret.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT COVERED, so the boundary is not rediscovered:
#
#   * A value on a different LINE from its keyword (`PASSWORD = (\n "..."\n)`).
#     rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §8.2 freezes matching as line-oriented - "a pattern
#     can never match across a newline" - so this is unreachable by any pattern
#     rule, not a gap in these patterns.  Pinned as the [[G01]]-[[G03]] cases in
#     tests/fixtures/sast-secret-forms/multiline.py so it stays visible.
#   * A quoted value CONTAINING SPACES (`password="correct horse battery"`).
#     Admitting spaces inside the quotes lets the value run from one string's
#     opening quote to a LATER string's quote on the same line, which is a false
#     positive that no context-deny can distinguish from a real one.
#   * A secret with no credential-naming identifier at all (`x = "hunter2"`).
#     That is entropy detection, a different mechanism; the vendored gitleaks
#     adapter (modules/sast/adapters/gitleaks/) is where that belongs.
#
# The guard block is SHARED across the four records on purpose - it is one
# policy about what a credential assignment is NOT, and four copies that drift
# are four different policies.  Each line is a shape that looks like an
# assignment but names a reference, a template, a path, a lookup, a boolean, a
# placeholder, or a metadata field (`PASSWORD_FILE`, `passwordMinLength`) rather
# than a credential.  Every one of them is pinned by a negative control.
#
# The block has two halves, and which half a guard belongs in is the point:
#
#   * `context-deny` lines describe the LINE around the match - a `${...}`
#     template, an `os.environ` lookup, a vault reference, a ternary.
#   * `match-deny` lines (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §10.1a) describe the MATCHED
#     ASSIGNMENT ITSELF - its name's metadata suffix, or its value's shape (a
#     path, a version range, a dotted module path, a placeholder).  These used
#     to be context-denies too, which at window 0 means "anywhere on the line":
#     an unrelated `://`, `=>` or `: true` later on the same line dropped a real
#     credential, and five of six realistic leaked credentials in one probe
#     file were dropped that way.
#
# The quoted secret and API-key records also refuse a value with NO DIGIT: an
# issued token or key always carries one, while a storage key, cookie name,
# enum, Redux action type or i18n string (`"auth_token"`, `"same-origin"`,
# `"Zugangstoken"`) does not.  The password record does not, because chosen
# passwords can be letters only.  tests/suites/sast-secrets-forms.sh section F
# checks the shared block and each record's permitted extras.
#
# Two path lists are shared the same way.  Localisation catalogues are
# excluded outright (a password key there holds the translated word for
# it, not a credential).  Test,
# documentation, example and CI paths are excluded from the keyword records
# and reported instead by SAST-SEC-NONPROD_CREDENTIAL-01 at `low`.
#
# The keyword records carry `severity-ceiling: high`.  Every secret-family
# finding sets sensitive_data, which the rubric raises by one step, so a
# medium-confidence keyword match would otherwise always read as CRITICAL; a
# CRITICAL is left to the high-confidence provider shapes (AWS key, PEM).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

id: SAST-SEC-AWS_AKID-01
title: Hardcoded AWS access key id
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-798
owasp: A07:2025
pattern: \b(AKIA|ASIA|ABIA|ACCA)[0-9A-Z]{16}\b
dialect: ere
context-deny: EXAMPLE|example|AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE|placeholder|XXXXXXXX
context-window: 0
tags: static
tags: quick
tags: compliance
remediation: Remove the key from source and rotate it immediately; a committed key must be
  treated as compromised even if the commit was reverted, because it remains in git history.
  Supply credentials through the instance role, the AWS profile, or the operator's secret store.
references: CWE-798
references: CIS-AWS-1.4
cis: 1.4

id: SAST-SEC-PRIVATE_KEY-01
title: Hardcoded private key
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-798
owasp: A07:2025
pattern: -----BEGIN ([A-Z]+ )?PRIVATE KEY-----
dialect: ere
context-require: (^[[:space:]#/*"'`>+|-]*[A-Za-z0-9+/]{40,}|PRIVATE KEY-----((\\r)?\\n)?[A-Za-z0-9+/]{40,})
context-window: 4
tags: static
tags: quick
tags: compliance
remediation: Rotate the key pair and remove it from source; a private key committed to a
  repository must be treated as compromised, including in reverted commits.
  Load keys from a secret store or an instance-role-backed credential provider instead.
references: CWE-798

# Any-case api/access key identifier, any quote style, quoted value of 16+
# bytes.  16 rather than the password rule's 6 because an API key is a
# machine-issued high-entropy string and a short one is far likelier to be a
# label than a credential.
id: SAST-SEC-GENERIC_API_KEY-01
title: Hardcoded API key literal
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-798
owasp: A07:2025
pattern: [A-Za-z0-9_-]*([Aa][Pp][Ii]|[Aa][Cc][Cc][Ee][Ss][Ss])[_-]?[Kk][Ee][Yy][A-Za-z0-9_-]*[\"']?[[:space:]]*[:=][[:space:]]*[\"'`][^\"'`[:space:]]{16,}[\"'`]
dialect: ere
context-deny: \$\{
context-deny: \$\(
context-deny: \{\{
context-deny: %\(
context-deny: <%
context-deny: (os\.environ|getenv|GetEnv|process\.env|System\.getenv|Deno\.env|ENV\[|environ\[|Environment\.Get)
context-deny: (vault|Vault|VAULT|secretsmanager|SecretsManager|secretKeyRef|valueFrom|from_secret|fromSecret|resolve:|ssm:|arn:aws:|keyring|Keychain|KeyVault|azure_key)
context-deny: \?[[:space:]]*["'][^"']*["'][[:space:]]+:[[:space:]]*["']
match-deny: [:=][[:space:]]*["'`]?\$
match-deny: [:=][[:space:]]*["'`]?[!~/.<>*-]
match-deny: [:=][[:space:]]*["'`]?(var|local|module|data|each|count|path|terraform|self|this|props|opts|args)\.
match-deny: [:=][[:space:]]*["'`]?(true|false|null|nil|none|yes|no|on|off|True|False|None|Nil|TRUE|FALSE|NULL|NIL|NONE|YES|NO|ON|OFF)\b
match-deny: ([Mm][Yy][-_]?([Pp][Aa][Ss][Ss]|[Ss][Ee][Cc][Rr][Ee][Tt]|[Tt][Oo][Kk][Ee][Nn])|[Cc][Hh][Aa][Nn][Gg][Ee][_-]?[Mm][Ee]|[Ee][Xx][Aa][Mm][Pp][Ll][Ee]|[Pp][Ll][Aa][Cc][Ee][Hh][Oo][Ll][Dd][Ee][Rr]|[Dd][Uu][Mm][Mm][Yy]|[Rr][Ee][Dd][Aa][Cc][Tt][Ee][Dd]|[Ss][Aa][Mm][Pp][Ll][Ee]|[Ss][Pp][Ee][Cc][Ii][Mm][Ee][Nn]|[Yy][Oo][Uu][Rr][_-]|[Ii][Nn][Ss][Ee][Rr][Tt][_-]|TODO|FIXME|XXXX|xxxx|\.\.\.)
match-deny: _(FILE|PATH|DIR|URL|URI|NAME|ID|ENV|VAR|HASH|DIGEST|REGEX|PATTERN|POLICY|LENGTH|MIN|MAX|ENABLED|DISABLED|REQUIRED|OPTIONAL|FIELD|HEADER|PARAM|PROMPT|LABEL|TITLE|TEXT|MESSAGE|TYPE|FORMAT|TTL|EXPIRY|EXPIRES|ROTATION|SUFFIX|PREFIX|COUNT|SIZE|LIMIT|ALGO|ALGORITHM|ENCODING|MASK|HINT|RESET|CONFIRM|ATTEMPTS|LOCKOUT|COLUMN|TABLE|SCHEMA|VERSION|STRENGTH|SCORE|AGE|HISTORY|REGION|ZONE|MTIME|TIME|DATE|TIMESTAMP)["'`]?[[:space:]]*[:=]
match-deny: _(file|path|dir|url|uri|name|id|env|var|hash|digest|regex|pattern|policy|length|min|max|enabled|disabled|required|optional|field|header|param|prompt|label|title|text|message|type|format|ttl|expiry|expires|rotation|suffix|prefix|count|size|limit|algo|algorithm|encoding|mask|hint|reset|confirm|attempts|lockout|column|table|schema|version|strength|score|age|history|region|zone|mtime|time|date|timestamp)["'`]?[[:space:]]*[:=]
match-deny: [a-z0-9](File|Path|Dir|Url|Uri|Name|Id|Env|Var|Hash|Digest|Regex|Pattern|Policy|Length|Min|Max|Enabled|Disabled|Required|Optional|Field|Header|Param|Prompt|Label|Title|Text|Message|Type|Format|Ttl|Expiry|Expires|Rotation|Suffix|Prefix|Count|Size|Limit|Algo|Algorithm|Encoding|Mask|Hint|Reset|Confirm|Attempts|Lockout|Column|Table|Schema|Version|Strength|Score|Age|History|Region|Zone|Mtime|Time|Date|Timestamp|At)["'`]?[[:space:]]*[:=]
match-deny: ([a-z0-9]PasswordKey|[Ee]xisting[A-Za-z0-9]*[Ss]ecret[A-Za-z0-9]*)["'`]?[[:space:]]*[:=]
match-deny: [:=][[:space:]]*["'`][\^~><=]*v?[0-9]+\.[0-9xX*]+(\.[0-9xX*]+)?(\.[0-9xX*]+)?([-+][0-9A-Za-z.-]*)?["'`]$
match-deny: [:=][[:space:]]*["'`](env\(|%[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*%["'`]$|\{[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_.]*\}["'`]$)
match-deny: [:=][[:space:]]*["'`][A-Za-z_]+\.[A-Za-z_]+\.[A-Za-z_.]*[A-Za-z_]["'`]$
match-deny: [:=][[:space:]]*["'`]([Mm]ock|[Ff]ake|[Ss]tub|[Tt]est)[-_]
match-deny: [:=][[:space:]]*["'`](required|omitempty)(,|["'`]$)
match-deny: [:=][[:space:]]*["'`][0-9]+/[smhd](/|,|["'`]$)
match-deny: [:=][[:space:]]*["'`][^0-9"'`]+["'`]$
